dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, emily. how are you doing today?
[patient] i'm doing good. how are you?
[doctor] i'm doing well, thanks. so, i see that you're here for your annual follow-up. you were switched to wixela inhaler. how's that working for you?
[patient] it's been working good.
[doctor] okay. so, you were on symbicort before, and you were switched to wixela. how's that working for you?
[patient] yeah, it's working good.
[doctor] okay. and you use the zyrtec during the spring, summer, and fall for your seasonal allergies. how's that working for you?
[patient] yeah, it works good.
[doctor] okay. and you have a peanut and tree nut allergy. you're trying to avoid those. do you have any issues with almonds and cashews?
[patient] no, i don't.
[doctor] okay. so, you're okay with almonds and cashews. how about tree nuts?
[patient] no, i'm okay with those too.
[doctor] okay. and you were trying to join the military, and they need you to be able to exert yourself without needing any inhalers. so, you're trying to wean off of them. how's that going?
[patient] yeah, i've been trying to.
[doctor] okay. so, you use the albuterol before you run, but otherwise, you're not using it. and then you're just using the wixela three times a week, one puff each day, and then the other days you use it twice a day as prescribed. is that right?
[patient] yeah, that's right.
[doctor] okay. and you haven't had to go to the urgent care or the emergency room for any respiratory issues in the past year, and you haven't had to use any steroids for breathing issues in the past year. is that right?
[patient] yeah, that's right.
[doctor] okay. and you're trying to join the military in the early spring of 2022. and you're doing some exertional testing to that purpose. and you have six to seven months to show that you can exert yourself without needing any inhalers. is that right?
[patient] yeah, that's right.
[doctor] okay. so, have you been vaping or smoking?
[patient] i have been vaping and smoking.
[doctor] okay. and are you trying to quit?
[patient] yeah, i'm trying to.
[doctor] okay. and have you cut down on that?
[patient] yeah, i've cut down.
[doctor] okay. and do you have a nebulizer?
[patient] yeah, i do.
[doctor] okay. and do you use it?
[patient] no, i don't need it.
[doctor] okay. you only used it in the past for upper respiratory infections, and a few times as a child. is that right?
[patient] yeah, that's right.
[doctor] okay. so, you use zyrtec during the spring and fall, but you don't have to use it consistently during the summer. how about in the summer?
[patient] yeah, i use it when i work outside.
[doctor] okay. and how often do you use it during the summer?
[patient] i use it three times a week.
[doctor] okay. and does it help with your symptoms?
[patient] yeah, it does.
[doctor] okay. and you have been successfully avoiding peanuts. is that right?
[patient] yeah, i have.
[doctor] okay. and do you remember that incident where you were exposed to peanuts when you were cleaning an enterprise rental car that had a lot of peanuts in it?
[patient] yeah, i do.
[doctor] okay. and what happened?
[patient] i developed contact hives when the peanuts touched my skin.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any other accidental exposures to peanuts since then?
[patient] no, i haven't.
[doctor] okay. and you wear long sleeves and change your gloves between each cleaning, and you think it's likely that you have been in contact with cars that contained peanuts, but you haven't touched them directly. is that right?
[patient] yeah, that's right.
[doctor] okay. and do you always keep an epi pen with you?
[patient] yeah, i do.
[doctor] okay. and how about tree nuts? do you avoid those?
[patient] yeah, i do.
[doctor] okay. and have you been able to tolerate any tree nuts?
[patient] yeah, i can tolerate almonds and cashews.
[doctor] okay. and do you avoid them?
[patient] yeah, i do.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any issues with almonds or cashews?
[patient] no, i haven't.
[doctor] okay. and do you avoid all tree nuts?
[patient] yeah, i do.
[doctor] okay. so, i'm just going to go over a few things. your asthma control test, your act score is twenty-one. that's a good score. and your current food skin test sensitivities include peanuts. and you have moderate persistent asthma, uncomplicated. allergic rhinitis, and anaphylactic reaction due to peanuts. so, i'm going to recommend that you start alvesco, two puffs twice a day. and if that doesn't work well, you can always go back to the wixela. and you can continue your albuterol as needed. and we'll repeat the act and spirometry in four months. so, do you have any questions or concerns?
[patient] no, i don't.
[doctor] okay. and i will refill your epi pen today. do you have any other questions or concerns?
[patient] no, i don't.
[doctor] okay. so, we'll see you back in four months. do you have any other questions or concerns?
[patient] no, i don't.
[doctor] okay. take care.
[patient] thank you. }}<|eot_id|>
