[doctor] okay. and then after that, you've had pain since then?
[patient] yes, since then.
[doctor] okay. and where is the pain located?
[patient] it's in the back of my shoulder.
[doctor] okay. is it in the front or the back of your shoulder?
[patient] the back.
[doctor] okay. and is it numb or tingling?
[patient] no, it's just pain.
[doctor] okay. and is the pain constant or does it come and go?
[patient] it's constant.
[doctor] okay. and have you been able to lift anything heavy since the injury?
[patient] no, i've been trying to avoid lifting anything.
[doctor] okay. and are you able to touch your shoulder to your ear?
[patient] no, i can't.
[doctor] okay. and are you able to move your shoulder at all?
[patient] yeah, i can move it, but it hurts.
[doctor] okay. and have you taken any pain medication?
[patient] i've taken tylenol.
[doctor] okay. does it help?
[patient] no, it doesn't.
[doctor] okay. have you had any fever or chills since the injury?
[patient] no, no fever or chills.
[doctor] okay. and did you hit your head when you fell?
[patient] yes, i did.
[doctor] okay. did you pass out?
[patient] no, i did not.
[doctor] okay. have you had any problems with your neck or any other joints?
[patient] no, just my shoulder.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any injuries to your right shoulder before?
[patient] no, never.
[doctor] okay. well, let's go ahead and do a physical exam of your neck and your shoulder. i'm just going to press on your shoulder a little bit. okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. so, i'm going to press on the back of your shoulder here. does that hurt?
[patient] yes, it does.
[doctor] okay. and i'm going to press on the front of your shoulder here. does that hurt?
[patient] yes, that hurts too.
[doctor] okay. and i'm going to bend your shoulder forward and backward. does that hurt?
[patient] yes, that hurts.
[doctor] okay. and i'm going to lift your arm up. does that hurt?
[patient] yes, that hurts.
[doctor] okay. and i'm going to lift your arm down. does that hurt?
[patient] yes, that hurts.
[doctor] okay. and i'm going to turn your arm in. does that hurt?
[patient] yes, that hurts.
[doctor] okay. and i'm going to turn your arm out. does that hurt?
[patient] yes, that hurts.
[doctor] okay. i'm going to press on your neck here. does that hurt?
[patient] no, it doesn't.
[doctor] okay. i'm going to feel your radial pulse here. that feels good. so, i'm going to go ahead and review your x-ray results. i did see your x-ray today, and it looks like you have a fracture of your acromioclavicular joint. this is the joint where your collarbone meets your shoulder blade.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and i think you're going to need to wear a sling for the next couple of weeks to help stabilize your shoulder. i'm also going to prescribe you meloxicam 15 milligrams once a day to help with your pain. i'm also going to order a ct scan of your shoulder to see if there's any other injuries that we need to look at.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and i'm also going to recommend that you attend formal physical therapy to strengthen your right shoulder. i want to try to avoid surgery if possible.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and i'm also going to advise you to avoid tackle football.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] does that sound good?
[patient] yes, it does.
[doctor] okay. i'm going to have my nurse come in and get you checked in and get you set up with your prescriptions and everything. do you have any questions?
[patient] no, i think that's it.
[doctor] okay. great. i'll see you in about 20 minutes. }}<|eot_id|>
