dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hi, how are you doing today?
[patient] I'm doing okay. I've been having some problems with my knee.
[doctor] Okay. So, what's going on with your knee?
[patient] I stepped off a curb and twisted my ankle about two and a half weeks ago.
[doctor] Okay. And where exactly is the pain?
[patient] It's on the outside of my right knee.
[doctor] Alright. And is it painful when you walk?
[patient] No, it's not painful when I walk, unless I stand on one foot for a long time.
[doctor] Okay. How about when you stand on both feet?
[patient] No, it's not painful when I stand on both feet.
[doctor] Alright. What about when you stand on your heel?
[patient] Yes, that's when I feel the pain.
[doctor] Okay. Have you noticed any clicking?
[patient] Yes, I have been clicking with every step.
[doctor] Alright. Does it hurt when you bend your knee?
[patient] No, I can bend all the way down.
[doctor] Okay. What about when you stand for a long time?
[patient] It locks up.
[doctor] Alright. Have you noticed any numbness or tingling?
[patient] Yes, I've had numbness and tingling in my foot before, but this is not new.
[doctor] Okay. Have you tried any treatments for the pain?
[patient] Yes, I've been taking ibuprofen and icing it.
[doctor] Alright. How has that been helping?
[patient] It helps at night and in the morning.
[doctor] Okay. How about in the evening?
[patient] It helps in the evening as well.
[doctor] Alright. Do you take any other medications?
[patient] No, just ibuprofen.
[doctor] Alright. Do you do any exercises?
[patient] I bike for exercise.
[doctor] Okay. How about your job?
[patient] I'm in sales.
[doctor] Alright. Any other problems?
[patient] No, just my knee.
[doctor] Okay. So, let me go ahead and check your knee.
[patient] Okay.
[doctor] Alright, so on physical exam, I do appreciate some edema and a slight effusion in the right knee. I don't appreciate any pain with palpation of the right lateral knee, but I do appreciate a positive varus test. I'm going to go ahead and check the neurovascular status of your right lower extremity. I do appreciate a palpable dorsalis pedis and posterior tibialis pulse. Now, I'm going to check the range of motion of your right knee. I'm going to bend it, straighten it, and rotate it. Everything looks good. I'm going to check for any pain with a Lachman's test. That was negative. Now, I'm going to go ahead and review the x-rays of your right knee. The x-rays did not reveal any fractures, dislocations, or bony abnormalities. No other abnormalities were noted. Based on your examination and radiographic findings today, I think you have a right knee lateral collateral ligament strain. I'm going to go ahead and prescribe you meloxicam 15 milligrams to help with your pain. I'm also going to recommend that we do some formal physical therapy to strengthen your right knee. If your pain does not improve with physical therapy, I will recommend that we get an MRI. Do you have any questions?
[patient] No, I think that sounds good.
[doctor] Alright. We will get you set up with physical therapy and get you started with the meloxicam. Do you have any other questions?
[patient] No, I think that's it.
[doctor] Alright. Thank you, and I'll see you in a few weeks.
[patient] Thank you. }}<|eot_id|>
