dialogue is: {{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you doing today?

[patient]: Hi, I'm doing well, thanks.

[doctor]: Great. I'm glad to hear that. You're here for your annual exam, correct?

[patient]: Yes, that's right.

[doctor]: Okay. So, let's start with your medical history. You have a history of type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure. How have you been managing those conditions?

[patient]: Yeah, I've been taking my medication as prescribed and my blood pressure has been good. But my diabetes has been a bit tricky lately.

[doctor]: I see. How has it been tricky?

[patient]: Well, I recently started working at a bakery and I've been trying new recipes and eating more sweets. I know I shouldn't, but it's hard to resist.

[doctor]: I understand. It can be challenging to resist the temptation of sweets, especially when you're around them all the time. Have you noticed any changes in your blood sugar levels since you started working at the bakery?

[patient]: Yes, my blood sugars have been fluctuating a lot. I've been trying to cut back on the sweets, but it's hard.

[doctor]: Okay. Have you been taking your medication as prescribed, including the metformin?

[patient]: Yes, I've been taking it twice a day and haven't missed any doses.

[doctor]: Great to hear that. How about your sleep? Have you been sleeping well?

[patient]: Yes, I've been sleeping well. I can get a full 8 hours of sleep each night.

[doctor]: That's good to hear. Do you have any thoughts of self-harm or suicidal ideations?

[patient]: No, I don't have any thoughts like that.

[doctor]: Okay, thank you for letting me know. Now, let's do a quick physical exam. Let me check your neck. It looks supple without any swelling or lumps. I'm not hearing any abnormal sounds in your lungs either. Your heart sounds regular and there's a mild murmur, but that's not uncommon. Your pulses are good.

[patient]: Okay, everything sounds good.

[doctor]: Great. Your grip strength is equal bilaterally and you seem neurologically intact. Now, let's move on to your medical results. Your hemoglobin A1c is elevated.

[patient]: Oh, that's not good to hear.

[doctor]: Yes, it indicates that your diabetes is not under control. We need to work on getting that under control. You're currently taking metformin twice a day. I recommend you check your fasting blood glucose levels each morning and call them in about 2 weeks. We'll discuss further treatment recommendations when you come back in about 2 months.

[patient]: Okay, I'll do that.

[doctor]: Great. Your blood pressure seems to be stable, but we'll keep an eye on it. You're currently taking lisinopril 10 mg once daily. You'll continue with that.

[patient]: Alright.

[doctor]: Do you have any questions or concerns about your treatment plan?

[patient]: No, I think I understand everything.

[doctor]: Good. It's important to follow the plan and monitor your blood glucose levels. We'll see you in about 2 months to reassess. Do you have any questions or concerns before we wrap up?

[patient]: No, I'm good. Thank you.

[doctor]: You're welcome. Take care and see you in 2 months.

[patient]: Thanks, bye.

[doctor]: Bye. }}<|eot_id|>
