dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Good morning, how are you doing today?

[patient] Hi, I'm okay, thanks.

[doctor] Great. How are you feeling?

[patient] I've been having some joint pain.

[doctor] Oh, sorry to hear that. Can you tell me more about it?

[patient] It's in my left elbow. It's been really bothering me.

[doctor] I see. How long has this been going on?

[patient] It's been a few weeks now.

[doctor] Okay. Can you describe the pain you're experiencing?

[patient] It's stiff and painful. After I'm done working, it's really hard for me to flex and extend my left elbow. There's also some swelling on the back of my elbow.

[doctor] Hmm, that sounds uncomfortable. Do you have any numbness or tingling in your hands?

[patient] No, I don't.

[doctor] And how about weakness in your arm? Do you feel like it's weak?

[patient] Yes, there's some weakness, but it's not too significant.

[doctor] Alright. Have you had any fever or chills?

[patient] No, none of that.

[doctor] I see. You mentioned you have a history of epilepsy. How's that been going?

[patient] I haven't had any recent seizures. My last one was a year ago.

[doctor] Good to hear. You're still taking Keppra, right?

[patient] Yes, I am.

[doctor] That's good. How about your depression? How's that been going?

[patient] I started therapy last year and it's been helpful, but I still feel down and stressed. I'm trying to avoid medication for it.

[doctor] I see. No suicidal or homicidal ideations, though?

[patient] No, none of that.

[doctor] Okay. You also mentioned you had an episode of gastritis and were diagnosed with peptic ulcer disease. How's that been going?

[patient] I had a lot of stress last year and then had an endoscopy. It showed an episode of gastritis. I was diagnosed with peptic ulcer disease and started taking Protonix. Everything has been good since then.

[doctor] That's good to hear. Any chest pain, shortness of breath, abdominal pain, nausea, or vomiting?

[patient] No, none of that.

[doctor] You mentioned some medial nasal congestion from allergies. How's that been?

[patient] Yes, it's been a bit of a problem.

[doctor] Alright. Let's go over your systems real quick. How's your ears, nose, and throat been?

[patient] Just the medial nasal congestion.

[doctor] No chest pain or shortness of breath?

[patient] No.

[doctor] No respiratory issues?

[patient] No.

[doctor] Any musculoskeletal issues other than your elbow?

[patient] No, just my elbow.

[doctor] And psychiatric issues?

[patient] Yes, I have depression.

[doctor] Alright, let's do a physical exam. I'll check your left elbow. There's some erythema and edema. It's painful to the touch. It's also warm to the touch and painful with flexion and extension. I can feel your radial pulse, though.

[patient] Oh, that sounds bad.

[doctor] Don't worry, it's just an examination. We'll get some imaging to rule out any fractures.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] We did an x-ray and it didn't show any evidence of a fracture. We also did an EGD and it showed an episode of gastritis.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] Based on all this, I believe you have left olecranon bursitis caused by repetitive trauma.

[patient] What does that mean?

[doctor] It means your elbow has been inflamed and irritated due to repetitive strain. We'll start you on meloxicam 15 mg once a day to help with the pain and inflammation.

[patient] Alright.

[doctor] I'll also recommend using a pillow to rest your arm on and icing the area. Have you been doing anything to help with the pain so far?

[patient] No, not really.

[doctor] Okay, we'll start with the meloxicam and see how that helps. How about your epilepsy? You're still taking Keppra, right?

[patient] Yes, I am.

[doctor] That's good. You've been doing well on it and haven't had any recent seizures. We'll continue the Keppra 500 mg twice a day.

[patient] Alright.

[doctor] You mentioned you've been doing well with therapy for your depression. We'll start you on Lexapro 10 mg once a day.

[patient] Okay, that sounds good.

[doctor] And you're still on Protonix 40 mg once daily before meals for your peptic ulcer disease. How's that been going?

[patient] It's been good.

[doctor] Good to hear. Do you understand and agree with the recommended medical treatment plan?

[patient] Yes, I do.

[doctor] Great. We'll follow up in two weeks to see how everything is going. Do you have any questions or concerns?

[patient] No, I think you've covered everything.

[doctor] Alright, we'll see you in two weeks then. Take care.

[patient] Thank you, thank you.

[doctor] You're welcome. }}<|eot_id|>
