dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hi, how are you today?

[patient] Hi, I'm doing well. Thanks for seeing me.

[doctor] Great. I see you're here for your annual follow-up for asthma, allergic rhinitis, and food allergy. How has everything been since our last visit in 2020?

[patient] It's been good, actually. I've been doing better with my asthma and allergies.

[doctor] That's great to hear. I see you were switched to Wixela from Symbicort due to insurance changes. How have you been managing with that?

[patient] Yes, I was switched to Wixela. I've been using it once daily, 3 times a week, and the other days I use it twice a day as prescribed. It's been working well for me.

[doctor] That's good to hear. Have you noticed any improvements in your symptoms?

[patient] Yes, my symptoms have been under control.

[doctor] Excellent. You mentioned you're trying to join the military. How's that going?

[patient] Yes, I'm in the process. They require a certain fitness level without the use of a rescue or maintenance inhaler. So, I've been weaning off my inhalers.

[doctor] That's interesting. You mentioned you use albuterol before a run, but otherwise, you function without it. How's that been working for you?

[patient] Yes, it's been working well. I've been reducing my Wixela dose as I feel I don't need it as much.

[doctor] I see. You've also been using Zyrtec for your seasonal allergy symptoms. How often do you use it?

[patient] I use it in the spring, summer, and fall. It works well for me. During the summer, I only use it when I work outside, which is 3 days a week.

[doctor] That's helpful. You mentioned you have known peanut and tree nut allergies. How have you been managing those?

[patient] I try to avoid peanuts, but I've had an incident in the past where I was exposed to peanuts when I was cleaning an Enterprise rental car. It caused contact hives. Since then, I've been taking precautions like wearing long sleeves and changing my gloves between each cleaning. I think it's likely I've been in contact with cars that contained peanuts, but I haven't touched them directly.

[doctor] I see. You also mentioned you tolerate almonds and cashew. Have you had any issues with those?

[patient] No, I've been fine with almonds and cashews.

[doctor] Great. How about tree nuts? Do you avoid those as well?

[patient] Yes, I avoid all tree nuts.

[doctor] Okay. You also mentioned you've been smoking and vaping, but you're trying to quit. How's that going?

[patient] Yes, I've cut down significantly. I'm trying to quit.

[doctor] Good for you. Have you had any urgent care or emergency room visits for respiratory issues recently?

[patient] No, I haven't had any visits.

[doctor] And you haven't needed prednisone for breathing issues in the past year?

[patient] No, I haven't needed it.

[doctor] Alright. You mentioned you have a nebulizer, but you haven't needed it recently. Is that correct?

[patient] Yes, that's right. I only used it in the past for upper respiratory infections and a few times as a child.

[doctor] Okay. Let's review your current medications. You're taking Wixela Inhub 250 mcg-50 mcg/dose powder for inhalation, 1 puff 2 times daily in the morning and evening. You also have Proair HFA as needed, an EpiPen as needed, and Alvesco 80 mcg 2 puffs 2 times daily. Is that correct?

[patient] Yes, that's correct.

[doctor] Great. How about your Asthma Control Test (ACT) score? It was 21 last time. How has your asthma been controlled recently?

[patient] It's been under control.

[doctor] Excellent. Your current food skin test sensitivities include peanuts. Have you had any accidental exposures to peanuts recently?

[patient] No, I haven't had any recent exposures.

[doctor] Alright. Your plan is to continue with Wixela and Alvesco, and you'll continue to use your albuterol inhaler as needed. You'll also continue using Zyrtec as needed, primarily during spring and fall. Do you have any questions about your plan?

[patient] No, I understand.

[doctor] Great. We'll also refill your EpiPen today, as it expires annually. We'll see you back in 4 months for a repeat ACT and spirometry testing.

[patient] Okay, that sounds good.

[doctor] Alright. You'll start Alvesco, 2 puffs twice daily. Keep the Wixela in case Alvesco doesn't work well. Continue albuterol as needed. Do you have any other questions?

[patient] No, I'm good.

[doctor] Great. We'll see you in 4 months. Take care and keep following your treatment plan.

[patient] Thank you, I will.

[doctor] Goodbye.

[patient] Goodbye. }}<|eot_id|>
