dialogue is: {{ [doctor]: Good morning, how are you doing today?

[patient]: Good, thank you. I'm here for my follow-up on my perioral dermatitis.

[doctor]: Ah, yes, let's go over your current condition. How has your perioral dermatitis been since your last visit?

[patient]: It's been well-controlled, but it hasn't fully resolved. My pharmacist told me to reduce the doxycycline to once a day since it was improving, so I've been using it once a day for about a week now.

[doctor]: I see. So you've been using the doxycycline twice a day initially, and then reduced it to once a day based on your pharmacist's advice. How has your skin been responding?

[patient]: It hasn't gotten worse, but it hasn't gotten better either.

[doctor]: Okay. Have you noticed any drying of the skin with the sulfacetamide wash?

[patient]: No, my skin hasn't dried out at all. It's actually been much improved in terms of moisturization.

[doctor]: That's good to hear. You're using the sulfacetamide wash twice daily, and you apply the MetroCream immediately after washing, correct?

[patient]: Yes, that's right.

[doctor]: And you're not using any other lotions or products, such as Cetaphil lotion or soap, or Neutrogena wipes?

[patient]: No, I'm not using any of those.

[doctor]: I understand you didn't start using Elidel because it's not covered by your insurance and you were concerned about potential side effects from the medication.

[patient]: That's right. I didn't want to take the risk.

[doctor]: I understand your concerns. Let's review your current medications. You're currently using doxycycline cream, applying it twice a day, MetroCream, and sulfacetamide wash, which you apply topically twice daily. Is that correct?

[patient]: Yes, that's right.

[doctor]: I'll do a quick physical exam to check your skin. Let me just take a look. (examines the patient's skin) I see some faint erythematous papules on your bilateral medial cheeks and mild redness around and underneath your nostrils. Your skin type is 4 to 5 on the Fitzpatrick scale.

[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: Based on your exam and history, you have perioral dermatitis and rosacea, including ocular rosacea. Given the improvement but not full resolution, I recommend that you resume applying the doxycycline cream twice daily until you reach full resolution, and then reduce the application to once a day for an additional week.

[patient]: Okay, I can do that.

[doctor]: I also understand you won't be using Elidel due to the cost. I'll provide a prescription for Tacrolimus instead. We'll need to discuss the potential side effects of this medication. Are you comfortable with that?

[patient]: Yes, I'm willing to try it.

[doctor]: Great. You'll continue with the sulfacetamide face wash once a day. I also recommend using the Bruder mask as needed.

[patient]: Alright.

[doctor]: For your rosacea, including ocular rosacea, you'll continue using the MetroCream twice daily. I also suggest gently washing your eyelids once daily with a gentle cleanser and trying a gentle cream such as Vanicream.

[patient]: Got it, thank you.

[doctor]: If your symptoms worsen, please call the clinic. You'll have a follow-up appointment in 4 weeks. Do you have any questions or concerns?

[patient]: No, I think I understand everything. Thank you.

[doctor]: You're welcome. Take care and see you in 4 weeks.

[patient]: Thank you, I'll see you then. }}<|eot_id|>
