dialogue is: {{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you today?

[patient]: Hi, I'm doing okay, thanks. I've been having some back pain.

[doctor]: I see. Can you tell me more about the back pain you're experiencing?

[patient]: Sure. It started about four months ago when I was moving boxes out of my son's college dorm. I felt a tingling in my right toes and it lasted for about an hour.

[doctor]: Hmm, that sounds uncomfortable. Did the tingling resolve after that hour?

[patient]: Yes, it did. But after that, I felt like I couldn't support my upper body weight. I also felt weak in my legs when carrying heavy objects.

[doctor]: I understand. How about the pain in your lower back? Can you describe it to me?

[patient]: It's a sharp pain, especially when I turn a certain way or roll over in my sleep. It goes away if I reposition myself.

[doctor]: Okay. Have you seen anyone else for this pain?

[patient]: Yes, I went to urgent care. They referred me to get an MRI, but I haven't been able to get it done because of insurance issues.

[doctor]: I see. How have you been managing the pain so far?

[patient]: I've been using Tylenol, ibuprofen, heat, and ice. The ibuprofen seems a bit more helpful than the Tylenol, but it doesn't really relieve my symptoms.

[doctor]: That's helpful to know. Have you experienced any upset stomach with the ibuprofen?

[patient]: No, I haven't had any issues with stomach upset.

[doctor]: Great. You mentioned you tried physical therapy at your gym, but it didn't help. Can you tell me more about that?

[patient]: Yes, I did physical therapy at my gym, but it didn't provide any relief from the pain.

[doctor]: Okay. How about any other symptoms like fever, chills, or bladder or bowel issues?

[patient]: No, none of those.

[doctor]: Alright, let's review your medical history. You mentioned you had a right total knee arthroplasty two years ago. How's your knee been doing since the surgery?

[patient]: It's been 95% better. I'm really happy with the results.

[doctor]: That's great to hear. Now, let's move on to your vital signs. Your temperature is normal, your blood pressure is 117/56 mm Hg, your heart rate is 68 BPM, and your oxygenation is 95% on room air. Everything looks good there.

[patient]: That's good to know.

[doctor]: Now, let's do a physical exam. When I examine your neck, I notice a bony protuberance at the C5-C6 area, but it doesn't cause any pain when I palpate it. Your lumbar spine is also normal to the touch. However, I do notice some decreased flexion and extension of your lower back. You also have a positive straight leg raise and some spasm in the paraspinal muscles of your lumbar spine. Your strength is equal bilaterally.

[patient]: Okay, that's what I've been feeling.

[doctor]: Alright. We've also reviewed your x-rays of the lumbar spine, and they didn't show any acute bony abnormalities. Based on your symptoms and the physical exam, I suspect you might have an acute disc herniation. I'd like to order an MRI to confirm this.

[patient]: Alright, that sounds like the next step.

[doctor]: Yes, an MRI will give us a better idea of what's going on. In the meantime, I've prescribed you meloxicam 15 mg to help with the pain. You'll also continue with physical therapy, which I've referred you to today. Does that sound okay?

[patient]: Yes, that sounds good. Thank you.

[doctor]: You're welcome. Do you have any questions or concerns?

[patient]: No, I think that covers everything.

[doctor]: Alright, we'll get the MRI scheduled and follow up with you once the results are in. Take care and feel better soon.

[patient]: Thank you, doctor. I appreciate it.

[doctor]: You're welcome. Take care. }}<|eot_id|>
