dialogue is: {{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you today?

[patient]: Hi, I'm okay, but I've been having some issues.

[doctor]: Oh, I see. What seems to be the problem?

[patient]: I've been having shortness of breath and I've been coughing up some blood.

[doctor]: Hmm, that doesn't sound good. Have you had any fever?

[patient]: No, I haven't had a fever.

[doctor]: Okay, and have you noticed any bleeding from your gums or any other areas?

[patient]: No, no bleeding.

[doctor]: Alright. How about your respiratory symptoms? Any wheezing or cough?

[patient]: Yes, I've been having shortness of breath and coughing up blood.

[doctor]: I see. Have you had any pain in your knees or lower extremities?

[patient]: Yes, I've had a long history of knee pain.

[doctor]: Okay. Have you had any bruising anywhere on your body?

[patient]: No, no bruising.

[doctor]: Alright. Let's take a look at your vital signs. Your pulse oxygenation is 94 percent and your blood pressure is 120/70 mm Hg. How about you're not feeling any chest pain or discomfort?

[patient]: No, I don't feel any chest pain.

[doctor]: Great. Let's do a physical exam. Your neck is supple, no jugular venous distension, no carotid bruits. Now, let's listen to your lungs. I hear some rales and rhonchi, mostly on the right side, but no wheezing. There's also some diminished breath sounds on the right side.

[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: Next, I'll listen to your heart. It has a regular rate and there's a 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, unchanged from before. Now, let's check your abdomen. It's soft. Next, I'll check your lower extremities. I see some 1+ nonpitting edema on both legs, but no calf tenderness and no Homan's sign.

[patient]: Alright.

[doctor]: I see. Any other medical conditions you're currently managing?

[patient]: I have diabetes and hypertension.

[doctor]: Okay. Have you been taking any medications?

[patient]: Yes, I take metformin and Norvasc.

[doctor]: Alright. Based on your symptoms and the physical exam, we need to do some further testing to determine the cause of your shortness of breath. I've ordered a chest x-ray, a CT scan, and blood work. You'll need to go to the ER immediately after this appointment, and I'll call ahead to let them know you're coming.

[patient]: Okay, I'll do that.

[doctor]: Great. In addition, your blood sugar hasn't been well controlled. I've ordered an updated A1c today. I'll increase your metformin to 750 mg twice daily.

[patient]: Alright, I'll follow that.

[doctor]: Also, your blood pressure has been running low for about three weeks. I think this could be related to your shortness of breath. We need to get a better understanding of the cause of your shortness of breath before pursuing further treatment for your hypertension.

[patient]: I understand.

[doctor]: I'll have you discontinue the use of Norvasc for now.

[patient]: Okay, I'll stop taking it.

[doctor]: Great. We'll have you follow up in about three weeks to discuss your diabetes and further treatment recommendations.

[patient]: Alright, I'll be back.

[doctor]: Great. Take care and see you soon.

[patient]: Thank you, doctor.

[doctor]: You're welcome. }}<|eot_id|>
