dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Good morning, how are you doing today?

[patient] Hi, I'm doing alright, thanks. I'm here because my knee hurts.

[doctor] I see. What seems to be the nature of your knee pain?

[patient] It's been bothering me for a few days now. I was playing pickup soccer games and felt a pop in my right knee.

[doctor] Hmm, a pop in your knee can be quite concerning. Can you tell me more about what happened?

[patient] Yes, it was 3 days ago. I was playing soccer and I felt a sudden pop in my knee. It was quite painful and swollen at the time.

[doctor] I see. The swelling has improved, you say? How is it now?

[patient] It's improved a bit, but it's still there. My knee isn't as functional or moving as well as it used to be.

[doctor] That sounds uncomfortable. On a scale of 0 to 10, how would you rate your pain level?

[patient] It's around a 6 or 7 out of 10. When the swelling goes down a bit, it's more like a 4 or 5.

[doctor] I see. Have you been doing anything to manage your pain?

[patient] Yes, I've been taking ibuprofen regularly, which helps a bit.

[doctor] Okay. Can you describe what happens when you try to move your knee? Does it hurt or feel stiff?

[patient] When I try to bend my knee, it hurts towards the end of the motion. When I go to straighten my knee, it feels like I have to help it straighten.

[doctor] Hmm, that sounds like it might be affecting your movement. Are you able to bear weight on your right knee?

[patient] Yes, I can bear weight on it, but it feels loose and doesn't feel normal.

[doctor] I see. Do you have any other symptoms or any other parts of your body that hurt besides your knee?

[patient] No, just the knee.

[doctor] Alright, let's go through a quick review of your systems. How about your job? Do you work at a desk?

[patient] Yes, I do.

[doctor] Okay. Have you noticed any issues with your work or daily activities due to your knee pain?

[patient] Yes, it's been affecting my work. I'm having trouble sitting and standing for long periods.

[doctor] I understand. Let's do a quick physical exam. Can you walk for me?

[patient] Sure.

[doctor] Okay, I see you have a marked limp in your right leg when you walk. How does that feel?

[patient] It feels like I'm favoring my left leg.

[doctor] Alright, let's examine your knee. There's some swelling and ecchymosis, or bruising, on the outside. Does that hurt when I press on it?

[patient] Yes, it hurts when you press on it.

[doctor] Okay, I'll check your range of motion. Can you bend your knee for me?

[patient] Sure.

[doctor] Hmm, you're lacking a few degrees of terminal extension, and it's painful. And when you try to bend it, you're lacking about 20-30 degrees due to pain. I also notice you have some pain when I apply pressure to the medial joint line.

[patient] Yes, that hurts too.

[doctor] Alright, let's do a quick test. I'll do a Lachman's test. Can you lie down and let me do this?

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] Alright, I'll do the test. It's positive, which suggests there might be some damage to the anterior cruciate ligament.

[patient] Oh, that doesn't sound good.

[doctor] I understand it's concerning, but we can run some tests to confirm. An MRI will give us a better idea of what's going on with your knee.

[patient] Okay, what's the next step?

[doctor] I've prescribed meloxicam 15 mg daily to help reduce the swelling and pain. I also recommend you use ice and modify your activities to reduce the swelling and pain. You should wear a knee immobilizer and use crutches when walking.

[patient] Alright, that sounds reasonable.

[doctor] Additionally, I advise you avoid jogging until we get the results of the MRI.

[patient] Okay, I'll do that.

[doctor] We'll follow up once the MRI results are available. Do you have any questions or concerns?

[patient] No, I think you've covered everything.

[doctor] Great. I'll see you once we get the MRI results. We'll discuss the next steps then.

[patient] Alright, thank you.

[doctor] You're welcome. Take care and see you soon. }}<|eot_id|>
