dialogue is: {{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you doing today?

[patient]: Hi, I'm okay. Thanks for seeing me.

[doctor]: Not a problem at all. I understand you're here for an evaluation of right heel pain. Can you tell me a bit more about how this started?

[patient]: Yes, sure. The pain started about 10 days ago. I've been training for a triathlon and I think it's related to that.

[doctor]: Ah, a triathlon. That's quite a challenging activity. Where exactly do you feel the pain in your heel?

[patient]: It's on the back of my heel, and it radiates up into my calf.

[doctor]: I see. Does the pain get worse when you do certain activities or at any other specific times?

[patient]: Yes, it gets worse when I'm working out and when I first get up in the morning. If I sit for a long time, it returns when I stand up.

[doctor]: That sounds quite uncomfortable. How often do you engage in these activities?

[patient]: I run 10 kilometers in the morning, and then at lunchtime, I try to bike for about 45 minutes to an hour. In the evening, I go to the aquatic center and swim for 30 minutes to an hour. I run 5 days a week, and on weekends, I alternate between a long run and a long bike ride. I take one day off from my exercises.

[doctor]: Wow, you're quite the athlete. Do you stretch at all?

[patient]: No, I don't stretch at all.

[doctor]: Alright, let's go through some other systems. Do you have any pain in your left leg?

[patient]: No, no pain there.

[doctor]: Great. Now, I'm going to do a physical exam. I'll start with your musculoskeletal system. Can you tell me if you have any pain when I do this?

[patient]: Yes, I do. It hurts when you press on the back of my heel.

[doctor]: Okay, let's examine the heel. There's some pain with resisted extension, and mild pain with flexion. I'm also going to do the calcaneal squeeze test. Can you bear with me?

[patient]: Sure.

[doctor]: Okay, I'm going to squeeze your heel now. It's a bit tender. There's also severe tenderness to the Achilles tendon. The Thompson test is negative, bilaterally.

[patient]: What does all that mean?

[doctor]: It means that your Achilles tendon is inflamed. This is likely due to overuse from your triathlon training. I've explained to you that it's best to take at least a week off from your training to recover. I'll also provide a referral for formal physical therapy to strengthen your right lower extremities.

[patient]: Okay, that sounds good. What else?

[doctor]: I'm also prescribing you 3 tablets of ibuprofen every 6 hours for pain. If your pain doesn't improve with physical therapy, we'll consider an x-ray.

[patient]: Alright, I'll do that.

[doctor]: Great. I'd like you to follow up with me in 7 to 10 days to check on your progress. Do you have any questions or concerns?

[patient]: No, I think I understand everything.

[doctor]: Good. We'll see you in a week or so. Take care and feel better.

[patient]: Thank you, I will. Bye.

[doctor]: Bye, take care. }}<|eot_id|>
