dialogue is: {{ [doctor] Hi there, how are you doing today?

[patient] Hi, I'm okay, thanks. I've been having some issues with my foot.

[doctor] Oh, I see. Can you tell me more about what's been going on with your foot?

[patient] Sure. I've had this non-healing ulcer on my right foot for about a year now.

[doctor] I see. When did this start?

[patient] It started about a year ago. I think it was just a blister at first, but then it turned red and I picked at it.

[doctor] Hmm, okay. Did you see a doctor about it initially?

[patient] Yes, I saw my primary care physician. They prescribed me Keflex.

[doctor] That's good. Did the Keflex help with your symptoms?

[patient] It did improve my symptoms, but it didn't completely resolve them.

[doctor] I see. When was the last time you took Keflex?

[patient] It was about 5 months ago.

[doctor] Alright. How has your pain been?

[patient] Actually, I don't have any pain.

[doctor] That's good. But you mentioned some other symptoms. Can you tell me more about the decreased sensation and increased drainage you're experiencing?

[patient] Yes, my foot doesn't feel like it used to. It's like there's no feeling there. And the drainage is pretty bad.

[doctor] I see. Have you been wearing your regular shoes?

[patient] Yes, I have.

[doctor] Okay. Have you noticed any changes in your blood sugar levels?

[patient] My blood sugars have been running a bit better than they were, but they're still in the 200's.

[doctor] That's good to hear. Have you had any recent lab work done, such as a hemoglobin A1C?

[patient] Yes, my last hemoglobin A1C was 10.

[doctor] Thank you for sharing that with me. Let's go through a few more things. How have you been feeling overall? Any fatigue?

[patient] Yes, I've been feeling quite fatigued.

[doctor] Alright. Now, let's do a quick review of your systems. Any fever?

[patient] No, I don't have a fever.

[doctor] Good. How about your skin? Any issues there?

[patient] I have this right foot ulcer and it's draining.

[doctor] Okay, I'll take a look. Let's get your vitals. Your temperature is normal, and your blood pressure and heart rate are within normal limits. Let's move on to the physical exam. Can you show me your foot?

[patient] Sure.

[doctor] I see a 1.0 cm x 2.0 cm wound on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot, just proximal to the 5th MTP joint. There's some yellow slough present with minimal granulation tissue. No surrounding erythema or cellulitis. I don't see any fluid collection. There's no evidence of necrosis. No odor, and no bony exposure. No pain to palpation.

[patient] Okay.

[doctor] I've also ordered an X-ray of your right foot. The results show no evidence of osteomyelitis.

[patient] Alright.

[doctor] Based on the findings, I'm going to recommend an ankle brachial index to determine your blood supply and see if we can heal the wound. We can also do a debridement in the office today, which you've agreed to.

[patient] Yes, that's fine.

[doctor] I've also prescribed collagenase ointment for you to apply to your wound once a day and cover it with a sterile dressing. You'll need to continue this until we see the ulcer decrease in size.

[patient] Alright, I can do that.

[doctor] Additionally, I recommend you be placed in a surgical shoe to provide increased support. This will help with the healing process.

[patient] Okay, I can do that.

[doctor] We'll need to follow up in 2 weeks to check on your progress. How does that sound?

[patient] That sounds good.

[doctor] Great. Do you have any questions or concerns?

[patient] No, I think that's it.

[doctor] Alright. I'll see you in 2 weeks. Take care and don't hesitate to reach out if you have any issues.

[patient] Thank you, I will.

[doctor] You're welcome. Take care.

[patient] Bye.

[doctor] Bye. }}<|eot_id|>
