dialogue is: {{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you doing today?

[patient]: Hi, I'm doing okay, but I've been having some chest pain on exertion.

[doctor]: I see. Can you tell me more about the chest pain? How often does it occur?

[patient]: It happens whenever I do any physical activity. It's a sharp pain and it lasts for a while after I stop.

[doctor]: Have you noticed any other symptoms along with the chest pain?

[patient]: No, just the pain.

[doctor]: Have you had any history of heart disease or hypertension?

[patient]: I haven't been formally diagnosed with hypertension, but my blood pressures have been trending high.

[doctor]: Okay. Have you been diagnosed with any other medical conditions?

[patient]: I have diabetes type 2.

[doctor]: That's important to note. Do you take any medications for your diabetes?

[patient]: Yes, I take metformin 500 mg twice daily.

[doctor]: Alright. Have you had any family history of heart disease?

[patient]: Yes, my family members have had heart disease.

[doctor]: I see. Let's go through a review of your systems. How have you been feeling generally? Any diaphoresis or clamminess?

[patient]: No, I haven't had any of those symptoms.

[doctor]: How about any chest pain at rest?

[patient]: Only when I exert myself.

[doctor]: Do you have any shortness of breath or difficulty breathing?

[patient]: No, I don't have any shortness of breath.

[doctor]: Any nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea?

[patient]: No, none of those either.

[doctor]: Great. Let's do a physical exam. Let me check your neck. Do you see any swelling or carotid bruits?

[patient]: No, I don't see any swelling or bruits.

[doctor]: Okay, let's check your respiratory system. How is your breathing?

[patient]: It's normal.

[doctor]: I'll listen to your lungs now. They sound clear bilaterally. Any wheezes, rales, or rhonchi?

[patient]: No, I don't hear any of those.

[doctor]: Let's move to your cardiovascular system. How's your heart rate and rhythm?

[patient]: It's normal.

[doctor]: I'll listen to your heart now. I hear a 2/6 systolic ejection murmur. Any murmurs, gallops, or rubs?

[patient]: No, I don't hear any of those.

[doctor]: Your pulses are equal. Let's check your abdomen. Any masses or tenderness?

[patient]: No, it's soft and there's no tenderness.

[doctor]: Any swelling or tenderness in your ankles?

[patient]: Yes, there's some swelling in my ankles.

[doctor]: Any calf tenderness?

[patient]: No, there's no tenderness.

[doctor]: Alright, let's order an EKG. We'll review that in the office. Your hemoglobin A1c is 7.0.

[patient]: Okay.

[doctor]: Based on your history of diabetes, possible history of hypertension, and family history of heart disease, I suspect your chest pain may be unstable angina. Your recent EKG was unremarkable, but there are no other signs of a heart attack. I'm worried you may have some sort of blockage causing the pain.

[patient]: I see. What should I do if I have the pain again?

[doctor]: We discussed proper protocol for sublingual nitroglycerin for chest pain. If you take the medication and it's not effective after about 5 minutes, I advise you to go to the emergency department.

[patient]: Alright.

[doctor]: We're also going to start you on baby aspirin at 81 mg daily. I've sent in a prescription for that. You'll also receive a prescription for nitroglycerin up to 3 tablets every 5 minutes as needed. Additionally, I'll have you follow up with cardiology for further evaluation.

[patient]: Okay, I understand.

[doctor]: How have you been managing your diabetes?

[patient]: I've been doing okay, just taking my metformin as prescribed.

[doctor]: Good. We'll have you continue with metformin 500 mg twice daily and dietary modifications. We'll also order a repeat hemoglobin A1c to check for any needed medication adjustments. You'll follow up in 2 weeks.

[patient]: Alright.

[doctor]: I also noticed you've had elevated blood pressures in your chart. Several providers have noted this. I'll have you monitor your blood pressures at home over the next 2 weeks and report your readings to me via MyChart. This will help us determine if any adjustments need to be made to your new medications. We'll also start you on hydrochlorothiazide at a low dose to help reduce some of the swelling you've noticed on physical exam.

[patient]: Okay, I'll do that.

[doctor]: Do you have any questions about the medications or the plan?

[patient]: No, I think I understand.

[doctor]: Great. Do you agree with the recommended medical treatment plan?

[patient]: Yes, I agree.

[doctor]: Alright, you'll follow up in 2 weeks. Remember to follow the instructions and monitor your blood pressures. If you have any questions or concerns, feel free to reach out to us.

[patient]: Thank you, doctor. I'll follow up in 2 weeks.

[doctor]: You're welcome. Take care and see you in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
