dialogue is: {{ [doctor]: Good morning, how are you today?

[patient]: Hi, I'm doing okay, thanks.

[doctor]: Great. I see you've been referred to me by your primary care provider. Can you tell me a bit about why you're here today?

[patient]: Sure. I've been having some pain in both of my knees.

[doctor]: Oh, I see. How long has this pain been going on for?

[patient]: It started about six months ago.

[doctor]: Hmm, six months. Do you remember any specific incident or injury that might have caused the pain?

[patient]: No, there was no specific incident. It just started.

[doctor]: Okay. How would you describe the pain?

[patient]: It's a deep, achy pain.

[doctor]: That sounds uncomfortable. Where exactly do you feel the pain in your knees?

[patient]: It's in the back of my patellae.

[doctor]: I see. Do you notice any swelling or redness in your knees?

[patient]: No, there's no swelling or redness.

[doctor]: Alright. Does the pain get worse at any particular times or activities?

[patient]: Yes, it gets worse when I transition from sitting to standing and when I go up or down stairs.

[doctor]: I understand. Does the pain go away if you rest?

[patient]: Yes, it does get better when I rest.

[doctor]: Okay. Have you been doing anything to try to manage the pain?

[patient]: Yes, I've been icing my knees and wrapping them with an ACE bandage. I also take ibuprofen or Aleve sometimes.

[doctor]: Alright. You mentioned you enjoy running. How far do you usually run?

[patient]: I usually run about 5 to 6 miles a day.

[doctor]: That's quite a distance! Have you noticed any changes in your running lately?

[patient]: Yes, I can only run about 0.5 miles now because of the pain.

[doctor]: I see. Have you had any injuries to your knees before?

[patient]: No, no previous injuries.

[doctor]: Okay. Do you work from home?

[patient]: Yes, I do.

[doctor]: That's convenient. Let's go over your medical history. Have you had any other health issues?

[patient]: I had an upper extremity fracture when I was in 6th grade.

[doctor]: I see. That's a while ago. How about your vital signs? Any changes there?

[patient]: No, they're all normal.

[doctor]: Alright. Let's do a quick physical exam. Can you walk for me?

[patient]: Sure.

[doctor]: Your gait looks normal, no deformity present. Let's check your heart. Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs. Any swelling or edema?

[patient]: No.

[doctor]: Good. Let's check your reflexes. Your patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical. Now, let's examine your lower extremities. Your abduction strength is 3/5, but the remainder of your muscle strength is 5/5. How does that feel?

[patient]: It feels normal, I think.

[doctor]: Alright. Let's examine your knees. No overlying erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin. No effusion. Can you feel any tenderness when I palpate the retinaculum?

[patient]: Yes, it's tender.

[doctor]: I'll do a patellar grind test. Can you feel this?

[patient]: Yes, it hurts.

[doctor]: And squatting causes pain?

[patient]: Yes, it does.

[doctor]: Okay. Let's check the Lachman test and anterior and posterior drawer tests. These are all negative. McMurray test is also negative.

[patient]: What does it all mean?

[doctor]: Based on your symptoms and the physical exam, it seems you have bilateral knee patellofemoral pain syndrome.

[patient]: What is that?

[doctor]: It's a condition where the pain is mainly in the front of your knees, specifically around the patellae. It's not due to any fractures or osteoarthritis, which we confirmed with X-rays.

[patient]: Okay. What can I do about it?

[doctor]: We'll start with conservative treatment. I recommend formal physical therapy to improve your lower extremity strength, mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics. You should take it easy on your running for now. You can continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatories as needed for pain.

[patient]: Okay, that sounds good.

[doctor]: Any questions about the treatment?

[patient]: No, I think I understand.

[doctor]: Great. We'll see you soon for a follow-up. Take care and follow the instructions.

[patient]: Thank you, I will.

[doctor]: You're welcome. Take care.

[patient]: You too.

[doctor]: Bye.

[patient]: Bye. }}<|eot_id|>
