dialogue is: {{ [doctor]: Hi there, how are you doing today?

[patient]: Hi, I'm doing okay. Thanks for seeing me.

[doctor]: Great, I'm Dr. Smith. I see you're here for an evaluation of your left knee. Can you tell me a bit about what's been going on?

[patient]: Sure. I've been having a lot of pain in my left knee. It started about a week ago when I was running around with my niece and I fell. I heard a pop and I think I twisted my knee.

[doctor]: Hmm, that sounds like it could have been quite painful. Can you describe the pain you're feeling?

[patient]: It's a throbbing pain. It's located on the medial and lateral sides of my knee.

[doctor]: I see. How would you rate the pain on a scale of 1 to 10?

[patient]: It's about a 4, but it's getting worse.

[doctor]: I see. Have you been doing anything to manage the pain?

[patient]: Yes, I've been taking Tylenol and wrapping my knee with kinesiology tape.

[doctor]: Okay, that's good that you're trying to manage the pain. Have you tried ibuprofen?

[patient]: No, I can't take ibuprofen because I have an ulcer.

[doctor]: Okay, I understand. Let's take a look at your knee. Can you tell me if you've had any other symptoms besides the pain?

[patient]: No, just the pain.

[doctor]: Alright. I'm going to examine your knee now. (examines the knee) I see there's some swelling and fluid around the knee. It looks like you have moderate range of motion. There's pain when you bend and straighten your knee. Let's do a few more tests. (performs Lachman's and valgus and varus stress tests) Everything looks normal on these tests.

[patient]: That's good, I guess.

[doctor]: Yes, it's looking good. Let's take a look at an X-ray of your knee. We'll take four views to get a better look. (shows the patient the X-rays) Do you see anything unusual on these?

[patient]: No, everything looks normal.

[doctor]: That's what I see too. The X-rays show no signs of a fracture or dislocation. Now, based on the exam and the X-rays, it looks like you might have a mild MCL strain. We'll treat this conservatively.

[patient]: Okay, what does that mean?

[doctor]: We'll put you in a knee immobilizer to provide extra support for the next week. This will help your knee heal faster. I've also prescribed tramadol 50 mg to help with the pain. You can take Tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain.

[patient]: Okay, that sounds good.

[doctor]: Also, I recommend icing your knee 3 to 4 times a day for 20 to 30 minutes each time. This will help reduce the swelling. And for the next 3 weeks, it's best to avoid running or any high-impact activities.

[patient]: Alright, I can do that.

[doctor]: If your pain doesn't improve with the immobilizer, we might need to get an MRI to see if there's any other damage. But let's start with this conservative treatment first and see how it goes.

[patient]: That sounds good. Thank you, Doctor.

[doctor]: You're welcome. Take care and follow up in a week. If you have any questions or concerns, feel free to call the office.

[patient]: Will do, thank you.

[doctor]: Alright, take care and see you soon. }}<|eot_id|>
