dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, ms. lee. how are you doing today?
[patient] i'm doing okay. i'm still having some back pain. but i'm really glad to be here.
[doctor] yeah, i'm glad to see you too. um, so i know you've had a pretty rough couple of days. um, can you tell me a little bit about what happened?
[patient] so, i was at a birthday party, and i was running up the stairs, and i ran into the railing, and i twisted my back, and i've been in a lot of pain ever since.
[doctor] okay. and where exactly did you hit your back?
[patient] so, um, i hit it right in the lower back, like, like the lumbar area.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any pain radiating down your legs?
[patient] no, not really.
[doctor] okay. and when you, when you, when you ran into the railing, did you feel a snap or a pop at all?
[patient] no, i did not.
[doctor] okay. and do you feel like you're able to move your back around at all?
[patient] not really. it's really sore. and when i lie down, it hurts a little bit more. and when i twist it, it hurts a lot more.
[doctor] okay. and how would you rate your pain on a scale of one to ten?
[patient] probably a seven.
[doctor] okay. and have you been taking any medication for the pain?
[patient] i've been taking ibuprofen. i took a few ibuprofen last night.
[doctor] okay. and when was the last time you took some?
[patient] um, i think it was last night. i took a few.
[doctor] okay. and have you tried using ice and heat?
[patient] i've tried using ice. i haven't tried using heat.
[doctor] okay. and how long have you been experiencing this pain for?
[patient] i've been experiencing it since last night.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any other medical problems?
[patient] i have anxiety.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any surgeries?
[patient] i had a lumbar fusion.
[doctor] okay. and when was that?
[patient] i think it was about six years ago.
[doctor] okay. and did you have any hardware put in?
[patient] yeah, i did.
[doctor] okay. and is the hardware still in?
[patient] yes, it is.
[doctor] okay. and is it still working okay?
[patient] yeah, it is.
[doctor] okay. well, let's go ahead and do a quick physical exam. i'm gon na check your back. does that hurt when i push on it?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any numbness or tingling in your legs?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. and when i bend you forward, does it hurt?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. and when i straighten you back up, does it hurt?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. and when i push on your back, does it hurt?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. and can you move your back around?
[patient] yes.
[doctor] okay. and does it hurt when you move?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. and are you able to straighten your legs out?
[patient] yes.
[doctor] okay. and are you able to bend them?
[patient] yes.
[doctor] okay. and when you bend them, does it hurt?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. and when you straighten them out, does it hurt?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any numbness or tingling in your legs?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any weakness in your legs?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. all right. let's go ahead and look at your x-rays. and your x-rays show that you had a lumbar fusion in the past. and the hardware is in good position. and, um, you don't have any signs of a fracture right now. so, um, based on your history and your physical exam, i think you have a lumbar strain. and i'm gon na prescribe you some ibuprofen, 800 milligrams three times a day with food. and i want you to use ice and heat. i don't want you to use heat at first. i want you to use ice for the first couple of days. and then i want you to switch over to heat. and i want you to take your ibuprofen three times a day with food. and if your pain doesn't get better, then we're gon na need to do some further testing. but i don't think that's necessary at this point. is that okay?
[patient] yeah, that sounds good. thank you.
[doctor] okay. is there anything else i can do for you?
[patient] no, that's it.
[doctor] okay. all right. take care.
[patient] thank you. }}<|eot_id|>
