dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, jordan, how are you?
[patient] i'm doing alright. i've been feeling a little anxious and my blood pressure's been running a little high.
[doctor] okay. so i see here that you were in the emergency room in march for palpitations and hypertension. is that correct?
[patient] yeah, that's right.
[doctor] and did you have any other episodes of palpitations?
[patient] no, not since then.
[doctor] okay. and have you noticed any other symptoms since then?
[patient] no, not really.
[doctor] okay. and how's your blood pressure been?
[patient] it's been running a little high. i've been checking it at home, it's been running between 146 and 155.
[doctor] okay. and have you been taking your medications?
[patient] i've been taking them, but i haven't been checking my blood pressure as regularly as i should.
[doctor] okay. and who's your primary care physician?
[patient] dr. smith.
[doctor] okay. and what medications is she having you on?
[patient] she put me on benicar hct and amlodipine.
[doctor] okay. and how long have you been on those?
[patient] since march.
[doctor] okay. and how are you feeling?
[patient] i'm feeling good. i'm just feeling a little anxious and my blood pressure's been running a little high.
[doctor] okay. and are you doing anything to help with your blood pressure?
[patient] i've been trying to reduce my salt intake.
[doctor] okay. and how's that going?
[patient] it's been going good. i've been watching what i eat and i've been walking on the treadmill a lot.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any family history of hypertension?
[patient] yes, my mother has it.
[doctor] okay. and how old was she when she was diagnosed?
[patient] she was diagnosed when she was in her early 50s.
[doctor] okay. and has she been on any medications for that?
[patient] yes, she's been on medications for it.
[doctor] okay. and how's she doing?
[patient] she's doing alright.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any other medical problems?
[patient] i've been having some problems with my thyroid.
[doctor] okay. and when was that diagnosed?
[patient] it was diagnosed about a year ago.
[doctor] okay. and how are you doing on that?
[patient] i've been doing good. i've been taking my medication and i've been feeling good.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any other medical problems?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay. and any other questions?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. well, let's do a quick physical exam. your heart rate is a little fast, but it's regular and it's in a regular rhythm. your heart sounds are normal. your lungs are clear. there's no edema. your abdomen is benign to exam. on your thyroid exam, it does feel a little bit enlarged, but that's consistent with your previous thyroid problems. on your thyroid profile from march, your tsh was a little bit high, but that was probably because you were on synthroid. and your potassium was a little low, but that's because you were taking a diuretic. and your sodium was a little low, but that's because you were taking a diuretic. so, overall, i'm a little concerned about your blood pressure. it seems to be resistant to the medications that we've been using. so, i'm going to add a medication called spironolactone to your regimen. i'm going to have you take that twice a day. i'm also going to have you increase your potassium intake. i'm going to have you follow up in two weeks to check your blood pressure and your basic metabolic panel. i'm also going to have you follow the dash diet and i want you to reduce your sodium intake to less than 2000 milligrams a day. i know that's a lot, but it's really important for your blood pressure. and i want you to increase your potassium intake as well. if your blood pressure is not better after you start the spironolactone, then we're going to have to consider adding another medication called carvedilol. i know that medication can cause some problems with your erections, but that's something that we can discuss in more detail when we see you in two weeks. i know that you're really trying to reduce your salt intake and you're doing a good job with that. i want you to just make sure that you're not getting dehydrated. if you start to feel dizzy or lightheaded, you need to drink some water. and if that doesn't get better, you need to come back and see me. i'm going to have my nurse come in and we're going to get you scheduled for that two week follow up. is that okay?
[patient] yeah, that sounds good.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any questions?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay. all right. we'll see you in two weeks.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. }}<|eot_id|>
