dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, how are you doing?
[patient] i'm doing okay, i'm just having some chest pain on exertion.
[doctor] okay. so, um, i understand that you have some chest pain on exertion. um, when you say on exertion, do you mean when you're walking or running or lifting?
[patient] um, it's like when i'm walking up the stairs.
[doctor] okay. and when you say you're walking up the stairs, do you mean like, you're walking up the stairs at home or like at work?
[patient] um, just at home.
[doctor] okay. and when you say you're having chest pain, what does that feel like? is it like a tightness or a heaviness or a burning sensation?
[patient] it's just a tightness.
[doctor] okay. and does it go to your arm or your jaw or your back?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. um, and when did this start?
[patient] um, a few days ago.
[doctor] okay. and how many days ago?
[patient] um, i'm not sure.
[doctor] okay. um, and how long does the pain last?
[patient] um, it's like, it just goes away when i stop.
[doctor] okay. and does it come back?
[patient] yeah.
[doctor] okay. and when you say you stop, do you mean you stop walking?
[patient] yeah.
[doctor] okay. and how long does the pain last after you stop?
[patient] um, i don't know, just a few minutes.
[doctor] okay. okay. um, and have you had any chest pain before?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. um, and how about shortness of breath? do you have any shortness of breath?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. and any nausea or vomiting or diarrhea?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any chest pain on lying down?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. um, and have you had any chest pain when you're sitting?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. um, and do you have a family history of heart disease?
[patient] yes.
[doctor] okay. and have you been diagnosed with high blood pressure?
[patient] i haven't been diagnosed with high blood pressure, but my blood pressures have been a little high.
[doctor] okay. and do you have diabetes?
[patient] yes, i have type two diabetes.
[doctor] okay. and do you have any other medical problems?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. um, and what kind of medication are you taking for your diabetes?
[patient] um, metformin.
[doctor] okay. and how much do you take?
[patient] 500 milligrams twice a day.
[doctor] okay. and any other medications?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. um, and have you had any other chest pain before?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. um, do you have any other medical problems?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. um, and any other medical problems?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. um, so let's do a quick physical exam. so, let me listen to your heart. i'm gon na listen to your heart and lungs. okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] so, i'm gon na listen to your heart. your heart is normal rate and rhythm, and i do hear a two over six systolic ejection murmur. your lungs are clear bilaterally, no wheezes, rales, or rhonchi. your neck is normal, no jugular venous distension or swelling, no carotid bruits. your abdomen is soft and nontender. your examination of your extremities shows one plus nonpitting edema in your ankles, no calf tenderness. so, let's talk about your chest pain. um, i think that you might be having some unstable angina, which is a blockage in one of your arteries that's causing the pain. um, i know you have a history of diabetes and you've had high blood pressures in the past. um, i know that you have a family history of heart disease. um, so i'm gon na put you on some medications. i'm gon na put you on baby aspirin at 81 milligrams daily. i'm gon na give you a prescription for nitroglycerin, three tablets every five minutes as needed, but i want you to go to the emergency room if the medication is not effective after five minutes. um, i'm also gon na have you follow up with cardiology for further evaluation. um, i'm gon na have you follow up in two weeks. um, let's talk about your diabetes. um, you're doing well on your current regimen. um, i want you to keep your appointment with ophthalmology for the evaluation of diabetic retinopathy. um, i'm gon na put in an order for a repeat hemoglobin a1c to check for any needed medication adjustments, and have you follow up in two weeks. um, i want you to continue with metformin 500 milligrams twice a day, and dietary modifications. um, let's talk about your high blood pressure. um, you have had high blood pressures in the past. um, i'm gon na have you start on hydrochlorothiazide at a low dose. um, i want you to monitor your blood pressures at home over the next two weeks, and report those to me via mychart. this should help us determine if any adjustments need to be made to your new medications. um, do you have any questions?
[patient] yeah.
[doctor] okay.
[patient] yeah.
[doctor] okay. go ahead.
[patient] so, i have a few questions. um, so, um, do you think this is like a heart attack or something?
[doctor] um, i think it's more like a blockage in one of your arteries that's causing the pain. um, i don't think it's a heart attack, but i do want you to go to the emergency room if the medication is not effective after five minutes.
[patient] okay. and then, um, what's the blockage? is it like, is it a clogged artery or?
[doctor] it's a blockage in one of your arteries.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. and what is the medication that you prescribed, uh, for the chest pain?
[doctor] um, i prescribed you some nitroglycerin, and you can take three tablets every five minutes as needed, but i want you to go to the emergency room if the medication is not effective after five minutes.
[patient] okay. and then, um, um, how will i know if it's a heart attack? like, what are the signs?
[doctor] um, if you're having chest pain, and you're having shortness of breath, and you're having nausea, and you're vomiting, and you're having diarrhea, then that's a heart attack. um, but if you're just having chest pain and you're not having any of those other symptoms, then it's probably a blockage in one of your arteries.
[patient] okay. and then, um, um, i have diabetes, and i have a family history of heart disease, so, um, what, like, what is the likelihood of me having a heart attack?
[doctor] um, i think you're at a higher risk for having a heart attack because you have a family history of heart disease, and you have diabetes. um, and you've had high blood pressures in the past. um, i think you're at a higher risk, but i don't think it's a high risk. um, i think it's more like a moderate risk. um, but i do want you to go to the emergency room if the medication is not effective after five minutes.
[patient] okay. and then, um, how do i get the medication?
