dialogue is: {{ [doctor] sophia, how are you?
[patient] i'm doing okay. i'm a little nervous.
[doctor] i know it's your first time coming in. i'm dr. taylor. i'm a physical medicine and rehabilitation doctor. i'm here to help you with your knee pain. so, how long have you been having this pain?
[patient] for about six months.
[doctor] okay. and how did you hurt it?
[patient] i don't know. i just woke up one morning and i was having pain in my knees.
[doctor] okay. and how would you describe the pain?
[patient] it's a deep, achy pain.
[doctor] okay. and where is the pain located?
[patient] in the back of my knees.
[doctor] okay. and have you noticed any swelling or redness?
[patient] no.
[doctor] okay. and how does it affect you?
[patient] it hurts when i walk up the stairs or down the stairs, or when i'm sitting down and then i stand up.
[doctor] okay. and have you been doing anything for it?
[patient] i've been icing it and wrapping it with an ace bandage. and i've been taking ibuprofen or aleve.
[doctor] okay. and what kind of activities do you do?
[patient] i run. i like to run.
[doctor] okay. how far do you run?
[patient] i usually run five to six miles a day.
[doctor] okay. and have you been able to run recently?
[patient] no, i haven't been able to run for a while.
[doctor] okay. and what about any other activities?
[patient] i try to be as active as i can. i like to walk and do other things.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any injuries to your knees before?
[patient] no, not that i'm aware of.
[doctor] okay. have you had any other injuries in your life?
[patient] i had a broken arm when i was in the sixth grade.
[doctor] okay. and do you work from home?
[patient] yes, i do.
[doctor] okay. so, have you had any other problems with your knees?
[patient] no, just this.
[doctor] okay. all right, so let's go ahead and do a quick physical exam. so, on your physical exam, your gait is normal. there's no deformity of your lower extremities. your cardiovascular exam is normal, no murmurs. your neurologic exam is normal. your musculoskeletal exam is as follows. your lower extremities are normal. your patella and achilles reflexes are symmetrical. your examination of your bilateral lower extremities, abduction strength is three out of five. the remainder of your muscle strength is five out of five. your examination of your bilateral knees, there is no overlying erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin appreciated. there is no effusion. there is tenderness to palpation of your bilateral retinaculum. your knee pain with squatting, negative lachman test, negative anterior and posterior drawer tests, negative mcmurray test. and on your x-rays of your bilateral knees, there is no evidence of any fractures or osteoarthritis. so, based on your exam and radiographic findings, i have diagnosed you with bilateral knee patellofemoral pain syndrome. so, i've had a lengthy discussion with you about your symptoms, and we've talked about your treatment options. i've recommended that we start with conservative treatment in the form of formal physical therapy to increase your lower extremity strength, mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics. i encouraged you to take running easy for now. you can continue to take ibuprofen or anti-inflammatories as needed for pain. all of your questions were answered. i want to see you soon, so we can see how you're doing and go from there. is that okay?
[patient] that sounds good. thank you.
[doctor] okay. }}<|eot_id|>
