dialogue is: {{ Doctor: Hi there. Can you confirm your name and date of birth for me please?
Patient: Um, my name is Thompson and my date of birth is October 1994.
Doctor: OK, thank you. Um, can you confirm your address and your contact details?
Patient: Yeah, I live at 123 Main Street, and my phone number is 555-5555.
Doctor: OK, thank you. Can you confirm your name and date of birth for me please?
Patient: Yeah, my name is Thompson and my date of birth is October 1994.
Doctor: OK, great. Um, can you tell me a bit more about what's been going on with your knee?
Patient: So, I've been swimming more regularly and trying to be more active, and I've noticed that my right knee has been hurting. Um, it starts hurting after a couple of laps. I can usually swim 10 laps, but now I'm getting pain in my knee. And I'm not sure what's going on.
Doctor: OK, um, have you had any injury or trauma to your knee that you're aware of?
Patient: No, I don't think so. I've just been swimming more and trying to be more active, and I've noticed the pain.
Doctor: OK, and is the pain in the front of your knee or the back of your knee?
Patient: Um, it's kind of in the front of my knee.
Doctor: OK, and is it worse when you're swimming or when you're walking?
Patient: Um, it's definitely worse when I'm swimming.
Doctor: OK, and is it worse when you're swimming with your arms or when you're kicking?
Patient: Um, it's definitely worse when I'm kicking.
Doctor: OK, and have you noticed any swelling or redness around the knee?
Patient: No, not that I've noticed.
Doctor: OK, and have you had any problems with your back or any other joints?
Patient: Um, I have chronic back pain, but it's been doing well lately.
Doctor: OK, and how long have you had that back pain?
Patient: Um, about a year or two.
Doctor: OK, and have you had any problems with your hands or wrists?
Patient: Yeah, I had carpal tunnel syndrome a couple of years ago, and I had surgery and it's been doing well.
Doctor: OK, great. Um, and have you had any problems with your depression?
Patient: Um, yeah, I have depression. But I've been doing really well with my medication and therapy.
Doctor: OK, great. And how's your sleep and your appetite been?
Patient: Um, I've been sleeping really well and eating really well.
Doctor: OK, great. And are you having any thoughts of hurting yourself or others?
Patient: No, I don't think so.
Doctor: OK, great. Um, can you do some straight leg raises for me? OK, can you lift your right leg up?
Patient: Yeah.
Doctor: And can you lift it up?
Patient: Yeah.
Doctor: OK, and how far can you lift it up?
Patient: Um, about this high.
Doctor: OK, and can you do it with your knee straight?
Patient: Yeah.
Doctor: OK, and is it painful?
Patient: Yeah, it's a little bit painful.
Doctor: OK, and can you lift your left leg up?
Patient: Yeah.
Doctor: And can you lift it up?
Patient: Yeah.
Doctor: OK, and how far can you lift it up?
Patient: Um, about this high.
Doctor: OK, and can you do it with your knee straight?
Patient: Yeah.
Doctor: OK, and is it painful?
Patient: Yeah, it's a little bit painful.
Doctor: OK, great. And can you walk on your heels?
Patient: Yeah.
Doctor: OK, and is it painful?
Patient: Yeah, it's a little bit painful.
Doctor: OK, great. Um, OK, OK, OK. Um, I think what's going on is that you've got some chondromalacia of your patella. So your patella's inflamed and that's causing you some pain. Um, and it's probably due to the fact that you've been swimming more and you've been kicking more. Um, and that's putting more stress on your patella and that's causing it to inflame.
Patient: OK.
Doctor: OK, so what I would recommend is that you try to reduce your swimming and reduce your kicking. Um, and if you can, you can try to swim freestyle instead of kicking. Um, and if you can, you can try to stop swimming all together. Um, and if you can't stop swimming, then I would recommend that you try to do some straight leg raises. Um, and that will help strengthen your quadriceps and that will help reduce your pain.
Patient: OK, OK.
Doctor: OK, and I would also recommend that you take some ibuprofen, um, 600 milligrams four times a day with food. Um, and that will help reduce your pain and inflammation.
Patient: OK.
Doctor: OK, and um, are you on any other medications?
Patient: Um, I'm on antidepressant medication.
Doctor: OK, OK, great. Um, and um, are you having any problems with your back?
Patient: Um, my back's been doing well lately.
Doctor: OK, great. And um, are you having any problems with your hands or wrists?
Patient: Um, my carpal tunnel's been doing well.
Doctor: OK, great. And um, do you have any other medical problems?
Patient: Um, no.
Doctor: OK, great. And um, do you have any other questions?
Patient: Um, no.
Doctor: OK, great. Um, OK, so I'm gonna prescribe you some ibuprofen and I would like you to come back in a couple of weeks and we can check on your knee. Um, and if you have any problems in the meantime, you can call me.
Patient: OK.
Doctor: OK, great. And um, is there anything else I can do for you today?
Patient: No, that's all.
Doctor: OK, great. Well, OK, take care. Bye bye.
Patient: Bye. }}<|eot_id|>
